  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT WED OCT 14 2026\n\nHigh temperatures remain in record territory today and tomorrow\ndue to an anomalous area of high pressure. Meanwhile, the\ninteraction of mid-level troughs (one near Alberta, Canada and\none near the Four Corners, U.S.A.) will nudge the high pressure\nsystem eastward, setting us up for the arrival of a refreshing\nfront.\n\nTemperatures will be a few degrees lower on Friday, but still\nabove what we'd expect for mid-October, before readings take a\ndive. From about mid Friday into the evening, the front is\nforecast to sweep across central Alabama. The front will be\nmoisture-starved, with forecast soundings indicating passing\nclouds and a limited prospect for showers. This is matched by a\nfew models that show scant precipitation echoes, which could end\nup being in the form of virga or a very light, brief shower at\nmost. By far the most noticeable effects from this front will be\nthe arrival of a breezy/gusty northwest to north wind and cooler\nair. The current forecast reflects sustained winds as high as\n20 mph with gusts near 30 mph, especially from mid Friday\nafternoon into Saturday. A lingering but not-as-windy east breeze\nis forecast for Sunday. Secure your Halloween decor! Weekend high\ntemperatures in the 60s (to near 70 for some) and low\ntemperatures in the 40s should satisfy fans of autumn.\n\nClear weather is currently projected heading into next week with\ntemperatures trending upward from the weekend, but near seasonal\nnormals.\n\n89^GSatterwhite",
